{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1149 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "54b9fd1f9d2313f6353345afa99bb7b276344d9d5b17d03a8efac785354d0117" :edb/db-before-t 1148 :edb/db-after-t 1149 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534461 50 #inst "2026-10-07T06:03:55.389Z" 13194139534461 true] [17592186092028 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010979/assets/834eff403ac2a6f696e104ec914cac1b951f8a6d989e11c0e9f2576ac9e7f0d8.png)\n\nFind an integral expression for the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1-t, \\quad y= \\dfrac{2}{3} t^{3/2}, \\qquad t\\in[0,1].\n$$" 13194139534461 true] [17592186092028 1100 "Find an integral expression for the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/997/Source/Images/c85ca457e7f02850924980f54ba585bf.png)\n\n$x=1-t, \\quad y= \\dfrac{2}{3} t^{3/2}, \\qquad t\\in[0,1]$." 13194139534461 false] [17592186092028 1145 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{ \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2})={t}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{(-1)}^{2}+{({t}^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534461 true] [17592186092028 1145 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\:\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(1 - t) =-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{2}{3}t^{3/2}) = t^{1/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\int _{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}\\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{1}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2} + (t^{1/2})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1 + t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534461 false] [17592186092029 1100 "Find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=e^t(\\cos{t}+\\sin{t}), \\quad y=e^t(\\cos{t}-\\sin{t}) , \\quad t\\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right].\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010979/assets/15d185178bf8882f837c19361d630c0e537e145ca2337cad4655677255d0348a.png)" 13194139534461 true] [17592186092029 1100 "Find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$x=e^t(\\cos{t}+\\sin{t}), \\quad y=e^t(\\cos{t}-\\sin{t}), \\quad t\\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right]$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/997/Source/Images/01cb59beb0b1df4a0cf0402429bafa19.png)" 13194139534461 false] [17592186092029 1145 "Computing the derivatives of $x$ and $y$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{t}(\\cos t + \\sin t)) \\\\\n&= e^{t}(\\cos t + \\sin t) + e^{t}(-\\sin t + \\cos t) \\\\\n&= 2e^{t}\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{t}(\\cos t - \\sin t)) \\\\\n&= e^{t}(\\cos t - \\sin t) + e^{t}(-\\sin t - \\cos t) \\\\\n&=-2e^{t}\\sin t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\int _{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}\\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(2e^{t}\\cos t)^{2} + (-2e^{t}\\sin t)^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{4e^{2t}(\\cos^{2} t + \\sin^{2} t)}dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}2e^{t}dt \\\\\n&= 2e^{t} \\mid _{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\\n&= 2(e^{\\pi /4} - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534461 false] [17592186092029 1145 "Computing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+{e}^{t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+{e}^{t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2({e}^{\\pi /4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534461 true] [17592186092060 1100 "Find the expression that gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=t, \\, y=\\ln{(\\cos{t})}$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (see graph below).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010979/assets/48de26c11ae08afb45ec19ee56e770f7418f9be2afb8ee7dd46a0d1136cb125b.png)" 13194139534461 true] [17592186092060 1100 "Find the expression that gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=t, \\, y=\\ln{(\\cos{t})}$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (see graph below).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/997/Source/Images/f10352be16212d467425c8695c797467.png)" 13194139534461 false] [17592186092060 1145 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{ \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+{tan}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534461 true] [17592186092060 1145 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\:\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t) = 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\ln (\\cos t)) =-\\tan t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= \\int _{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}\\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(1)^{2} + (-\\tan t)^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1 + \\tan^{2} t}dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{\\sec^{2} t}dt \\\\\n&= \\int _{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sec tdt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534461 false] [17592186092322 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289497 13194139534461 true] [17592186092322 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289518 13194139534461 true] [17592186092322 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289556 13194139534461 true] [17592186092322 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289563 13194139534461 true] [17592186092322 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289570 13194139534461 true] [17592186092323 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289504 13194139534461 true] [17592186092323 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289511 13194139534461 true] [17592186092323 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289525 13194139534461 true] [17592186092323 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289532 13194139534461 true] [17592186092323 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289539 13194139534461 true] [17592186092324 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289490 13194139534461 true] [17592186092324 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289546 13194139534461 true] [17592186092324 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289550 13194139534461 true] [17592186092324 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289553 13194139534461 true] [17592186092418 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534461 true] [17592186092418 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010979/assets/ac040bc2c3eb943f947f34602c7c03e90f18e129a0fa7749d8ae60faaab12f71.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139534461 true] [17592186092418 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/997/Source/Images/q-10255.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = t - \\sin t, y = 1 - \\cos t, t \\in  [0, \\pi ]$." 13194139534461 false] [17592186092418 1145 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the double-angle formula for cosine, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$ Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{t}{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{t}{2}) & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\ 2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =4{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2\\cdot {\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-4(0-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289490 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289491 13194139534461 true] [17592186289490 1089 "q-10256" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289490 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534461 true] [17592186289490 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010979/assets/da529910e268ea4c124d7c27b085ca1799e255604459c539cc8d5809dda8f95a.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={sin}^{3}⁡t,\\,y={cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289490 1113 #uuid "d83d6389-9d08-5064-9e29-4779ea6cc6fc" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289490 1145 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t)=3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t)=-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{4}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}(-1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289491 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289492 13194139534461 true] [17592186289491 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289493 13194139534461 true] [17592186289491 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289494 13194139534461 true] [17592186289491 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289495 13194139534461 true] [17592186289491 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289496 13194139534461 true] [17592186289491 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534461 true] [17592186289491 1041 "selection" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289491 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289496 13194139534461 true] [17592186289491 1110 #uuid "900b3e75-1dde-526a-904c-07a2130445fb" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289492 1034 "\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289492 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534461 true] [17592186289492 1111 #uuid "e651852e-a80d-57bf-b588-adb5465498ab" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289493 1034 "\\frac{4}{3}" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289493 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534461 true] [17592186289493 1111 #uuid "a16f8a58-4b38-5bd4-9523-9317cb156282" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289494 1034 "1" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289494 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534461 true] [17592186289494 1111 #uuid "9a458430-505e-51a9-96a9-ffe032c92552" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289495 1034 "\\frac{3}{4}" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289495 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534461 true] [17592186289495 1111 #uuid "750d5b53-2633-5cf7-aef4-0f5c4afa2113" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289496 1034 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289496 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534461 true] [17592186289496 1111 #uuid "1249