}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{144{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}12|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289547 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289548 13194139534461 true] [17592186289547 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289549 13194139534461 true] [17592186289547 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534461 true] [17592186289547 1041 "field-1" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289547 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289548 13194139534461 true] [17592186289547 1110 #uuid "dd8179b9-1599-5443-89d6-6c9a50d5f03f" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289548 1034 "3" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289548 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534461 true] [17592186289548 1111 #uuid "3d7e69ba-dfb4-54f1-967b-f46bb90aec38" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289549 1034 "6" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289549 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534461 true] [17592186289549 1111 #uuid "782a60a8-7501-5dba-b015-682ebcab6500" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289550 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289551 13194139534461 true] [17592186289550 1089 "q-351474" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289550 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534461 true] [17592186289550 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010979/assets/cf06561072376949c6df49aec94b45c57b1cd43fbb031ea7b157ea5dfbac877a.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{9}(6t+1)^{3/2},\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289550 1113 #uuid "232b62ea-7d8c-5d44-a709-a25bd20b0e4d" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289550 1145 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{9}(6t+1)^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(6t+1)^{1/2}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =(6t+1)^{1/2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{((6t+1)^{1/2})}^{2}+(3t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{6t+1+9{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{(3t+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}|3t+1|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2}+t)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2}+1)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289551 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289552 13194139534461 true] [17592186289551 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534461 true] [17592186289551 1041 "field-1" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289551 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289552 13194139534461 true] [17592186289551 1110 #uuid "9415ae20-ebe6-54df-b8ea-70a5aecd7046" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289552 1034 "\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289552 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534461 true] [17592186289552 1111 #uuid "193db4fa-521b-5423-9b5a-a047c541b60c" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289553 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289554 13194139534461 true] [17592186289553 1089 "q-351505" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289553 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534461 true] [17592186289553 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010979/assets/893efa56ec41ef2242a48831ad4f58b23edc791464ccc7dcb4dd104b87843360.png)\n\nFind the arc length $L$ of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=t-\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,t\\in [0,3].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289553 1113 #uuid "5db1ec2e-c404-5e8a-8537-4e63d5b3ba7e" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289553 1145 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =1-{t}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}) \\\\  & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{(1-{t}^{2})^{2}+(2t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1-2{t}^{2}+{t}^{4}+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}+{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\sqrt{(1+{t}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(1+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3})\\,{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =(3+\\frac{27}{3})-0 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289554 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289555 13194139534461 true] [17592186289554 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534461 true] [17592186289554 1041 "field-1" 13194139534461 true] [17592186289554 1104 #edb/ref 175921862